"tree":[3.54,-53.03,13,5]},{"tree":[-49.5,-7.07,13,5]},{"tree":[-24.75,-38.89,13,5]}],"notes":"Munson-Williams-Proctor Arts Institute Museum of Art, Utica (Philip Johnson, 1960): a square box of dark granite, windowless above, lifted over a recessed glazed ground floor in a dry moat, held by an exposed bronze-clad frame — two piers on each face carried over the roof as crossing beams; the entrance bridge from Genesee Street. Beside it Fountain Elms (1850), the family's Italianate brick house with its low hipped roof and bracketed eaves. The model is turned 45 degrees to stand square to Genesee Street as the ground file shows. Written from general knowledge; guessed: the box's size (about 38 m square, 17 m high), the pier spacing, Fountain Elms' position and the link. No photographs were available."}